    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129177",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem",
      "problem": "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{x-3}?$ \n\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[-5,-1]$\n- $(-5,3)$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[1,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=3.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-5,-1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-5,-1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-5,3)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
