    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-131069",
      "knowledge_point_id": "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{8}^{x}-61, & x<2 \\\\ {3}^{x-1}, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[1,3]$\n- $(-1,1)$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[3,5].$ In this interval, $f(x)={3}^{x-1},$ so the function is continuous on the interval $[3,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[1,3].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}({8}^{x}-61)={8}^{2}-61=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}{3}^{x-1}={3}^{2-1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=2$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,3],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
